Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** for catching the moment a style asks for an image that the map does not yet know about, and then supplying a replacement (or a real one) **using only MapLibre‑GL‑JS’s event system**.  
The pattern works for any version ≥ 1.15 (the `styleimagemissing` event was added in 1.13).

---

## 1. Why the event exists

When a style JSON contains a `layout.icon-image` (or `layout.pattern`) that refers to an image name that has **not** been added to the map’s style image registry, MapLibre throws a **`styleimagemissing`** event instead of silently failing.  

* The event payload contains the **`id`** (the name the style asked for) and a **`type`** (`icon` or `pattern`).  
* The map will **pause rendering** of any layer that depends on that image until you either add the image or tell it to ignore the request.

That makes it perfect for a “just‑in‑time” loader or a graceful fallback.

---

## 2. Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Missing‑icon handler</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (no custom images added yet)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        osm: { type: 'raster', tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'], tileSize: 256 }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' },
        // This layer *asks* for an icon that does not exist yet.
        {
          id: 'points',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: {
            type: 'geojson',
            data: {
              type: 'FeatureCollection',
              features: [
                { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [0, 0] }, properties: {} }
              ]
            }
          },
          layout: {
            // The style references an image called "my‑pin". It is NOT added yet.
            'icon-image': 'my-pin',
            'icon-size': 1.2
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Listen for the missing‑image event
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('styleimagemissing', async (e) => {
    console.warn(`🛑 Image "${e.id}" is missing – trying to load it now…`);

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Decide how to obtain the image.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    //   a) Try to fetch it from a known URL pattern
    //   b) If that fails, fall back to a tiny placeholder (a red circle)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    const url = `https://example.com/icons/${e.id}.png`; // <-- adapt to your host

    try {
      // `loadImage` returns a Promise in recent MapLibre builds.
      const image = await map.loadImage(url);
      map.addImage(e.id, image);
      console.info(`✅ Added "${e.id}" from ${url}`);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`❌ Could not load "${e.id}" from ${url}. Using fallback.`);

      // ---- Fallback: a 32 × 32 red circle drawn on a canvas ----
      const size = 32;
      const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
      canvas.width = canvas.height = size;
      const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
      ctx.fillStyle = '#ff5555';
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(size / 2, size / 2, size / 2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();

      // The canvas element can be used directly as an ImageData source.
      map.addImage(e.id, canvas);
      console.info(`✅ Added fallback image for "${e.id}"`);
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Force the style to re‑evaluate now that the image exists.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // The map automatically re‑paints after `addImage`, but if you
    // have a custom source that loads later you may want to call:
    //   map.triggerRepaint();
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  (Optional) Global error listener – useful for debugging
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('error', (e) => {
    // e.error contains the original Error object.
    console.error('MapLibre error event →', e.error);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| **1️⃣** | Creates a map with a style that references an image called `"my-pin"` – but we never call `map.addImage('my-pin', …)` up‑front. |
| **2️⃣** | Registers a listener for `styleimagemissing`. This fires **once per missing id** (if the same id is requested again after you add it, the event will not fire). |
| **3️⃣** | Tries to load the image from a predictable URL (`https://example.com/icons/<id>.png`). If the request fails (404, CORS, network error, etc.) we fall back to a tiny programmatically‑generated canvas. |
| **4️⃣** | Calls `map.addImage(e.id, …)`. Adding the image automatically un‑blocks the style and the symbol layer is rendered. |
| **5️⃣** | A generic `error` listener is added so you can see any other MapLibre‑GL‑JS errors (e.g., malformed style JSON). |

