## 1️⃣ What actually happens when an icon is missing?

When the style is parsed MapLibre walks through every `layout.icon-image` (or `paint.icon-color`, `text-field`, etc.) and asks the **style image manager** for the image with that name.

* If the image **exists** – great, it is used.
* If the image **doesn’t exist** – MapLibre emits a **`styleimagemissing`** event **and** falls back to a transparent 1 × 1 pixel so the layer still renders (but you see a blank spot).

That event is your hook to react, load the missing asset, and then tell the map “hey, I added it, draw again”.

---

## 2️⃣ The event‑driven solution

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Create the map (any version ≥ 2.0.0 works)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: 'https://example.com/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Listen for the missing‑image event
map.on('styleimagemissing', async (e) => {
  // e.id is the name the style asked for (e.g. "my‑pin‑icon")
  console.warn(`🛑 Icon "${e.id}" is missing – trying to load it…`);

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2a️⃣  Decide how you want to obtain the image.
  //      Three common strategies are shown below.
  // -------------------------------------------------

  // ──► 2a‑i. Load from a known URL pattern
  // (you can also keep a map of id → URL if you need custom logic)
  const url = `https://my.cdn.com/icons/${e.id}.png`;

  try {
    const img = await loadImage(url);          // ← see helper below
    map.addImage(e.id, img);
    console.info(`✅ Added "${e.id}" from ${url}`);
    return;                                     // done!
  } catch (err) {
    console.warn(`❓ Could not fetch "${url}" – falling back`);
  }

  // ──► 2a‑ii. Generate a placeholder on‑the‑fly (e.g. a red circle)
  const placeholder = generatePlaceholder(e.id);
  map.addImage(e.id, placeholder);
  console.info(`✅ Added placeholder for "${e.id}"`);
  // (no return – we already added the image)

  // ──► 2a‑iii. If you have a pre‑loaded sprite sheet, pull from there
  // (MapLibre already does this for the built‑in sprite, so you only need
  //  this if you maintain your own sprite atlas.)
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Helper: load an image from a URL and return an HTMLImageElement
function loadImage(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image();
    // Important for cross‑origin images used in WebGL
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
    img.onload = () => resolve(img);
    img.onerror = (e) => reject(e);
    img.src = url;
  });
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Helper: generate a tiny canvas‑based placeholder
function generatePlaceholder(name) {
  const size = 64;               // 64 × 64 px – enough for most icons
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = canvas.height = size;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

  // Background (transparent)
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, size, size);

  // Draw a red circle with the first letter of the id
  ctx.fillStyle = '#e53935';
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(size / 2, size / 2, size / 2 - 2, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fill();

  // Optional: put the first character of the id in the centre
  ctx.fillStyle = '#fff';
  ctx.font = `${size * 0.5}px sans-serif`;
  ctx.textAlign = 'center';
  ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';
  ctx.fillText(name.charAt(0).toUpperCase(), size / 2, size / 2);

  // Convert canvas → Image (MapLibre accepts HTMLImageElement)
  const img = new Image();
  img.src = canvas.toDataURL();
  return img;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  (Optional) Re‑paint the map once the image is added.
//     MapLibre automatically re‑renders when you call `addImage`,
 //    but if you need to force a style refresh you can do:
map.on('styleimagemissing', () => map.triggerRepaint());

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 6️⃣  If you want to be extra safe, also listen for generic errors
map.on('error', (e) => {
  if (e && e.error && e.error.message?.includes('Image')) {
    console.warn('MapLibre error:', e.error.message);
  }
});
```

### What the code does, step‑by‑step

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1** | Instantiates the map. | Nothing special – just a normal map. |
| **2** | `styleimagemissing` fires **once per missing id**. | This is the only event that tells you *exactly* which icon the style tried to use. |
| **2a‑i** | Tries to fetch a PNG from a predictable URL. | Works for most static icon sets (`my‑pin‑icon.png`, `restaurant.svg`, …). |
| **2a‑ii** | If the fetch fails, we fall back to a generated placeholder. | Guarantees the user never sees a completely blank spot. |
| **2a‑iii** | (Optional) Pull from a custom sprite sheet you already loaded. | Useful when you ship a sprite atlas with the app. |
| **3‑4** | Helper functions keep the main listener tidy. | `loadImage` returns a promise, `generatePlaceholder` gives you a quick fallback without any network request. |
| **5** | `triggerRepaint()` is optional because `addImage` already forces a redraw, but it can be handy if you batch‑add many images. | Guarantees the map updates immediately. |
| **6** | Global `error` listener catches any other image‑related errors that might slip through. | Good for debugging in production. |

---

## 3️⃣ Why use the **event system** instead of pre‑loading everything?

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| **Pre‑load every possible icon** (e.g. `map.addImage` before `setStyle`) | Simple, no runtime surprises. | Bloats the initial payload; you may never use many of the icons. |
| **Listen to `styleimagemissing`** (the pattern above) | *Lazy* – only load what the style actually asks for. <br>Works with **dynamic styles** that change at runtime (e.g. user‑selected themes). <br>Graceful fallback for typos or missing assets. | Slightly more code, a tiny extra network round‑trip the first time an icon appears. |

In most modern web‑mapping apps the lazy‑load pattern is the sweet spot: you ship a tiny “fallback sprite” (or none at all) and let the map fetch the real icons on demand.

---

## 4️⃣ Edge Cases & Gotchas

| Situation | How to handle it |
|-----------|------------------|
| **Multiple layers request the same missing icon** | `styleimagemissing` fires **once per missing id**, not per layer, so you’ll only load the image a single time. |
| **Icon name contains a dash or colon** (`my-icon:active`) | The event’s `e.id` is exactly the string the style used, so you can map it to a URL however you like (e.g., replace `:` with `_`). |
| **SVG icons** | `loadImage` works for SVGs as long as they are served with `image/svg+xml` and have proper CORS headers. |
| **Very large icons** | MapLibre will automatically resize the image to the size defined by `icon-size` in the style. Still, keep your source files reasonably sized (< 256 px) for performance. |
| **Icon added after the style is already rendered** | `map.addImage` automatically marks the style as “dirty” and triggers a repaint, so the newly added icon appears instantly. |
| **You need a different DPI for retina screens** | Detect `window.devicePixelRatio` and load `@2x` assets when `> 1`. Example: `const suffix = window.devicePixelRatio > 1 ? '@2x' : ''`. |
| **You want to cache the fetched images yourself** | Store the `HTMLImageElement` (or its `ImageBitmap`) in a `Map<string, HTMLImageElement>` and reuse it on subsequent `styleimagemissing` events. |

---

## 5️⃣ Full‑featured example (including caching & retina support)

```js
const iconCache = new Map(); // id → HTMLImageElement

map.on('styleimagemissing', async (e) => {
  const id = e.id;
  if (iconCache.has(id)) {
    // Already loaded earlier – just re‑add it to the style manager.
    map.addImage(id, iconCache.get(id));
    return;
  }

  // Build a URL that respects devicePixelRatio
  const dpr = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
  const suffix = dpr > 1 ? '@2x' : '';
  const url = `https://my.cdn.com/icons/${id}${suffix}.png`;

  try {
    const img = await loadImage(url);
    iconCache.set(id, img);
    map.addImage(id, img);
    console.info(`✅ Loaded "${id}" from ${url}`);
  } catch (_) {
    // Fallback placeholder (generated once per id)
    const placeholder = generatePlaceholder(id);
    iconCache.set(id, placeholder);
    map.addImage(id, placeholder);
    console.warn(`⚠️ Using placeholder for "${id}"`);
  }
});
```

*The cache guarantees that even if the same missing icon appears in a later style change, you won’t hit the network again.*

