### TL;DR  
`maplibre-gl` is now shipped **as a pure ES‑module**.  
`require('maplibre-gl')` (CommonJS) will throw because the package’s `main` field points to an ESM entry point.  

**The supported ways to load it in a Node‑only environment (i.e. without a bundler) are:**

| Situation | What to do | Example |
|-----------|------------|---------|
| **Your project is already ESM** ( `"type":"module"` in `package.json` or you’re using `.mjs` files) | Use a normal static import. | ```js\nimport maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';\n``` |
| **You are in a CommonJS file but can use a dynamic import** (Node ≥ 12.20 / 14.13) | Use `await import()` (or the promise form). | ```js\n(async () => {\n  const { default: maplibregl } = await import('maplibre-gl');\n  // …use maplibregl here\n})();\n``` |
| **You must stay in pure CommonJS** (e.g. legacy code) | Load the **UMD build** that lives in `dist/`. It is a self‑contained script that works with `require`. | ```js\n// Note: this pulls in the UMD bundle, not the ESM entry point.\nconst maplibregl = require('maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.js');\n``` |
| **You want a “node‑only” version** (no browser globals) | Use the **native** package instead: `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-native`. It is built for server‑side rendering / image generation. | ```js\nimport maplibregl from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-native';\n``` |

Below you’ll find a short “how‑to” for each of those approaches, plus a few gotchas you’ll run into when you try to use `maplibre-gl` outside a browser.

---

## 1️⃣ Why `require('maplibre-gl')` now fails

Since **v2.0** (released 2022) `maplibre-gl` dropped its CommonJS entry point and ships only an **ESM** entry:

```json
// package.json of maplibre-gl v2.x
{
  "type": "module",
  "exports": {
    ".": "./dist/maplibre-gl.esm.js"
  }
}
```

When Node sees a `require()` for a package whose `package.json` has `"type":"module"` (or an `"exports"` field that points to an `.mjs/.js` file interpreted as ESM), it throws:

```
Error [ERR_REQUIRE_ESM]: require() of ES Module .../maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.esm.js from ...
```

That’s why the old `require('maplibre-gl')` no longer works.

---

## 2️⃣ Preferred: Use ES‑module syntax

If you can switch your codebase to ESM (the recommended path), it’s as simple as:

```js
// index.mjs  or any .js file when package.json has "type":"module"
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Example: create a map (you still need a DOM – see “Browser‑like environment” below)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map', // id of a <div> in your HTML or a jsdom element
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

**Node version** – any recent LTS (≥ 14.13) supports this out‑of‑the‑box.

---

## 3️⃣ If you must stay in CommonJS: Dynamic import

Node lets you call `import()` from a CommonJS file. It returns a promise that resolves to the module namespace object.

```js
// index.cjs
(async () => {
  const { default: maplibregl } = await import('maplibre-gl');

  // now you can use maplibregl just like in an ESM file
  console.log('MapLibre version:', maplibregl.version);
})();
```

*Why the destructuring?*  
`import()` returns an object `{ default: …, … }`. The default export is the constructor you want.

---

## 4️⃣ Pure CommonJS fallback: the UMD bundle

The `dist/` folder contains a **UMD** build (`maplibre-gl.js`) that registers itself on `window` (or `global` in Node) and also works with `require`. It is deliberately left as a CommonJS‑compatible entry point for environments that cannot use ESM.

```js
// index.cjs
const maplibregl = require('maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.js');

// The UMD bundle attaches the constructor to the export directly:
console.log('MapLibre version (UMD):', maplibregl.version);
```

> **Note:** The UMD bundle still expects browser globals (`window`, `document`, `navigator`). If you run it in a pure Node process you’ll need to provide a minimal DOM shim (e.g. `jsdom`) **or** use the native package (see next section).

---

## 5️⃣ “Node‑only” use‑case: `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-native`

If you are trying to **render maps on the server** (e.g. generate PNGs, do headless rasterisation, or run tests that don’t need a real browser), the regular `maplibre-gl` package is not the right tool – it is built for the browser and will throw if `window`/`document` are missing.

The project ships a **native** variant that works directly in Node:

```bash
npm install @maplibre/maplibre-gl-native
```

```js
// index.mjs (or .cjs with dynamic import)
import maplibregl from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-native';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  width: 800,
  height: 600,
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

await map.render();               // renders to an off‑screen GL context
const png = await map.getCanvas().toBuffer('image/png');
// …write png to disk, send over HTTP, etc.
```

The native package bundles a headless OpenGL context (via `node-gyp`/`glfw`), so you **don’t need a DOM** at all.

---

## 6️⃣ Quick checklist for “outside a bundler”

| ✅ | What you need to do |
|---|----------------------|
| **Set the module system** | Add `"type":"module"` to your `package.json` **or** use `.mjs` files. |
| **Import the library** | `import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';` (ESM) **or** `await import('maplibre-gl')` (CJS) **or** `require('maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.js')` (UMD). |
| **Provide a browser‑like environment** (only for the browser build) | Install `jsdom` and create a fake `window`/`document` if you really need to run the browser build in Node. |
| **If you only need server‑side rendering** | Use `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-native` instead of the browser package. |
| **Node version** | ≥ 14.13 (for static ESM) or ≥ 12.20 (for dynamic `import()`). |
| **Optional polyfills** | `node-fetch` (or `undici`) for `fetch`, `globalThis` for `window`, etc., if you’re using the browser bundle. |

