Below is a short, self‑contained guide that shows **exactly** how to pull the camera state you asked for from a MapLibre GL JS map:

* center (Lng/Lat)  
* zoom  
* bearing (rotation)  
* pitch (tilt)  
* the 4 × 4 projection matrix that the renderer uses internally  

---

## 1️⃣ The “public” getters (center, zoom, bearing, pitch)

MapLibre already ships a clean public API for the first four items:

```js
// Assume you already have a map instance
// const map = new maplibregl.Map({ … });

function getPublicCameraState() {
  const center  = map.getCenter();   // → maplibregl.LngLat object
  const zoom    = map.getZoom();     // → number
  const bearing = map.getBearing();  // → degrees, 0 = north up
  const pitch   = map.getPitch();    // → degrees, 0 = looking straight down

  return {center, zoom, bearing, pitch};
}
```

You can call this function any time (e.g. inside a `move`, `zoom`, `rotate`, or `render` event) and you’ll get the exact values that MapLibre is using for the view.

---

## 2️⃣ The projection matrix (the “secret” part)

The matrix that the WebGL renderer uses is **not part of the public API**, but it is exposed on the internal `map.transform` object.  
At the moment (MapLibre GL JS v2.x) the matrix lives in `map.transform._projMatrix` – a `Float32Array` of length 16 in **column‑major order** (the same layout WebGL expects).

> **⚠️** Because it is a private field (`_projMatrix`) it may change in a future release.  
> If you want a more future‑proof solution, use a *custom layer* (see §3) – the matrix is handed to you every frame.

### 2.1 Direct read (quick‑and‑dirty)

```js
function getProjectionMatrix() {
  // The matrix is updated *after* the map has rendered a frame.
  // If you need the freshest value, call this inside a 'render' listener.
  const raw = map.transform._projMatrix;   // Float32Array(16)

  // If you prefer a plain JS array (e.g. for console.log or math libraries):
  const matrix = Array.from(raw);
  return matrix;
}
```

### 2.2 Putting it all together

```js
function getFullCameraState() {
  const {center, zoom, bearing, pitch} = getPublicCameraState();
  const projectionMatrix = getProjectionMatrix();

  return {
    center,          // maplibregl.LngLat
    zoom,            // number
    bearing,         // degrees
    pitch,           // degrees
    projectionMatrix // Array[16] (column‑major)
  };
}

// Example: log the state on every render tick
map.on('render', () => {
  console.log(getFullCameraState());
});
```

---

## 3️⃣ “Official” way to grab the matrix – via a **custom layer**

If you prefer to stay completely within the public API, create a tiny custom layer.  
MapLibre calls the layer’s `render(gl, matrix)` method on every frame and passes the **combined view‑projection matrix** (the same matrix you would otherwise read from `transform._projMatrix`).

```js
const cameraMatrixLayer = {
  id: 'camera-matrix-layer',
  type: 'custom',
  renderingMode: '3d',          // we need the 3‑D matrix, not the 2‑D one
  onAdd: function (map, gl) {
    // nothing to initialise
  },
  render: function (gl, matrix) {
    // `matrix` is a Float32Array(16) – column‑major, same as WebGL expects
    // Store it wherever you need it (e.g. a global variable, a closure, etc.)
    window.latestViewProjMatrix = Array.from(matrix);
  }
};

map.addLayer(cameraMatrixLayer);
```

Now `window.latestViewProjMatrix` will always contain the most recent matrix, and you can read the other camera parameters with the public getters shown in §1.

---

## 4️⃣ Bonus: Using the newer **FreeCameraOptions** API (MapLibre GL v2+)

MapLibre also ships a higher‑level “free‑camera” API that bundles the same values into a single object:

```js
function getFreeCameraState() {
  const opts = map.getFreeCameraOptions(); // → maplibregl.FreeCameraOptions
  // opts.center is a LngLat, opts.zoom a number, etc.
  return {
    center:  opts.center,
    zoom:    opts.zoom,
    bearing: opts.bearing,
    pitch:   opts.pitch,
    // No matrix here – you still need one of the two approaches above.
  };
}
```

This is handy if you already use `setFreeCameraOptions` elsewhere, but it still does **not** expose the matrix.

---

## 5️⃣ Quick‑start demo (copy‑paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – read camera state</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
    #info { position:absolute; top:10px; left:10px; background:#fff; padding:8px; font-family:monospace; max-width:300px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<pre id="info"></pre>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: 30
  });

  // ---- custom layer that captures the matrix each frame ----
  const matrixLayer = {
    id: 'matrix-capture',
    type: 'custom',
    renderingMode: '3d',
    onAdd: () => {},
    render: (gl, matrix) => {
      window._lastMatrix = Array.from(matrix); // store globally
    }
  };
  map.addLayer(matrixLayer);

  // ---- UI update -------------------------------------------------
  const info = document.getElementById('info');

  function updateInfo() {
    const center  = map.getCenter();
    const zoom    = map.getZoom().toFixed(2);
    const bearing = map.getBearing().toFixed(2);
    const pitch   = map.getPitch().toFixed(2);
    const matrix  = window._lastMatrix ? window._lastMatrix.map(v => v.toFixed(4)) : [];

    info.textContent = `
center : ${center.lng.toFixed(5)}, ${center.lat.toFixed(5)}
zoom   : ${zoom}
bearing: ${bearing}°
pitch  : ${pitch}°
matrix : [${matrix.join(', ')}]
`.trim();
  }

  // update on every render tick (ensures matrix is fresh)
  map.on('render', updateInfo);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, pan/zoom/rotate the map, and you’ll see the live camera state (including the 4 × 4 matrix) printed in the corner.

