## 1️⃣ Minimal HTML + JS Boilerplate

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – read camera state</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no">
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    #info { position:absolute; top:10px; left:10px; background:#fff; padding:8px; font:12px/1.4 monospace; max-width:300px; overflow:auto; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<pre id="info"></pre>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Create the map
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style:    'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
    center:   [0, 0],   // lon, lat
    zoom:     2,
    pitch:    45,
    bearing:  0,
    antialias: true,   // needed for the matrix to be up‑to‑date on every frame
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Helper: format a Float32Array (4×4 matrix) nicely
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  function formatMatrix(m) {
    // m is a Float32Array(16) in column‑major order (WebGL convention)
    const rows = [];
    for (let r = 0; r < 4; r++) {
      const row = [];
      for (let c = 0; c < 4; c++) {
        // column‑major → index = c*4 + r
        row.push(m[c * 4 + r].toFixed(5));
      }
      rows.push(row.join('  '));
    }
    return rows.join('\n');
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Function that reads *all* camera state at the moment it is called
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  function readCameraState() {
    // Public getters (documented API)
    const center   = map.getCenter();          // → maplibregl.LngLat
    const zoom     = map.getZoom();            // → number
    const bearing  = map.getBearing();         // → number (degrees)
    const pitch    = map.getPitch();           // → number (degrees)
    const projName = map.getProjection();      // → 'mercator' | 'globe' | …

    // The 4×4 projection matrix lives inside the internal Transform object.
    // It is a Float32Array(16) in *column‑major* order (exactly what WebGL expects).
    // The property is called `_projMatrix` in MapLibre GL JS 2.x‑4.x.
    const projMatrix = map.transform._projMatrix; // ← private but stable

    // If you ever need the *inverse* matrix you can compute it with gl‑matrix:
    // const inv = mat4.invert(mat4.create(), projMatrix);

    return {
      center:   { lng: center.lng, lat: center.lat },
      zoom,
      bearing,
      pitch,
      projection: projName,
      projMatrix,               // raw Float32Array
      projMatrixFormatted: formatMatrix(projMatrix) // nice string for UI
    };
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Show the state whenever the camera moves (drag, zoom, rotate, …)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  function updateInfo() {
    const state = readCameraState();
    document.getElementById('info').textContent = `
center   : ${state.center.lng.toFixed(5)}, ${state.center.lat.toFixed(5)}
zoom     : ${state.zoom.toFixed(3)}
bearing  : ${state.bearing.toFixed(2)}°
pitch    : ${state.pitch.toFixed(2)}°
projection: ${state.projection}

Projection matrix (column‑major):
${state.projMatrixFormatted}
`.trim();
  }

  // Initial display
  map.once('load', updateInfo);

  // Keep it up‑to‑date on every camera change
  map.on('move', updateInfo);
  map.on('pitch', updateInfo);
  map.on('rotate', updateInfo);
  map.on('zoom', updateInfo);
  map.on('render', updateInfo); // ensures we also catch programmatic changes

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  OPTIONAL: expose the state on the console for quick debugging
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  window.map = map;               // so you can type `map` in devtools
  window.readCameraState = readCameraState;
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the snippet does

| Step | What you get | How you get it |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Center** | `{lng, lat}` | `map.getCenter()` → `LngLat` object |
| **Zoom**   | `number`    | `map.getZoom()` |
| **Bearing**| `number` (°) | `map.getBearing()` |
| **Pitch**  | `number` (°) | `map.getPitch()` |
| **Projection name** | `'mercator'`, `'globe'`, … | `map.getProjection()` |
| **Projection matrix** | `Float32Array(16)` (column‑major) | `map.transform._projMatrix` (internal) |

All of the *public* getters (`getCenter`, `getZoom`, …) are part of the stable MapLibre GL JS API and will never break.  
The matrix lives in the **private** `transform` object.  It has been stable since the very first MapLibre GL JS release, but if you ever see a breaking change you can fall back to the *custom‑layer* technique described below.

---

## 2️⃣  “What if I don’t want to touch a private field?”  

MapLibre GL JS gives you a **custom layer** hook that receives the exact same matrix that the renderer uses.  If you prefer a fully‑public API, just add a tiny no‑op custom layer and read the matrix from its `render` callback:

```js
// 1️⃣  Define a dummy custom layer that only stores the matrix
const matrixReader = {
  id: 'matrix-reader',
  type: 'custom',
  renderingMode: '2d',          // or '3d' – doesn't matter for us
  onAdd: function (map, gl) {
    this.map = map;
    this.lastMatrix = null;
  },
  render: function (gl, matrix) {
    // `matrix` is a Float32Array(16) – the same one we accessed privately.
    this.lastMatrix = matrix;
  }
};

// 2️⃣  Add it to the map (it will be called every frame)
map.addLayer(matrixReader);

// 3️⃣  Whenever you need the matrix:
function getProjectionMatrixViaCustomLayer() {
  return matrixReader.lastMatrix; // may be null until the first render
}
```

*Pros*: No private property access, works even if the internal layout changes.  
*Cons*: You have to keep a custom layer alive (tiny overhead, negligible).

---

## 3️⃣  Quick cheat‑sheet of the most useful camera‑related APIs

| API | Returns | Typical use |
|-----|---------|-------------|
| `map.getCenter()` | `LngLat` | Current lon/lat |
| `map.getZoom()` | `number` | Zoom level |
| `map.getBearing()` | `number` (°) | Rotation around north |
| `map.getPitch()` | `number` (°) | Tilt of the camera |
| `map.getProjection()` | `'mercator' \| 'globe' \| …` | Which earth model is active |
| `map.getFreeCameraOptions()` | `{center, zoom, bearing, pitch, padding}` | All options in one object (useful for cloning) |
| `map.setFreeCameraOptions(opts)` | – | Programmatic camera moves |
| `map.project(lngLat)` | `Point` (pixel coordinates) | World → screen |
| `map.unproject(point)` | `LngLat` | Screen → world |
| `map.transform._projMatrix` | `Float32Array(16)` | **Projection matrix** (internal) |
| `customLayer.render(gl, matrix)` | `matrix` param | **Projection matrix** (public) |

---

## 4️⃣  Why the matrix matters

The matrix you read is the **combined view‑projection matrix** that MapLibre GL JS uses for every vertex shader.  In mathematical terms:

```
clipSpace =  projMatrix × viewMatrix × worldPosition
```

* `projMatrix` = the value you read (`_projMatrix`).  
* `viewMatrix` is baked into that same matrix (MapLibre multiplies view × projection internally).  

If you need to transform a 3‑D point expressed in **world coordinates** (e.g. a `[lon, lat, altitude]` converted to Mercator meters) into **screen space**, you can simply multiply it by this matrix (after converting to homogeneous coordinates).  The matrix is already in the WebGL column‑major layout, so you can feed it straight into any GL‑matrix library (`gl‑matrix`, `three.js`, etc.).

---

